# Test: a session whose peer stops reading is dropped, and the peer reconnects.
#
# THE ONE QUESTION: when an Established peer keeps its TCP connection open,
# keeps sending KEEPALIVEs and reads nothing, does the running daemon end that
# session and bring the peer back, so the routes reach it on a new connection?
#
# VALIDATES: spec-bgp-session-write-wedge AC-8. Every session write is bounded
# by the write budget (ze.fwd.write.deadline), so a write the peer never
# accepts fails, the session is retired, the run loop reconnects and the
# initial sync starts again.
#
# PREVENTS: the wedge. The config routes below are written by the initial
# sync, which gave its writes no deadline. With the peer's window shut the
# write blocked in the kernel holding the session's write lock, and every
# path that ends a session waited for that lock before closing the socket.
# The receive hold timer never fired, because the peer still sends
# KEEPALIVEs, and the send hold timer waits at least eight minutes and then
# wedged on the same lock. The session never ended.
#
# Why a .ci and not only the unit tests: the reactor unit tests hold the write
# path by hand on an in-memory pipe. They cannot show that a real kernel socket
# fills, that the budget set through the environment reaches the write that
# blocks, or that the daemon's own run loop dials again and re-sends its routes.
#
# HOW THE PEER STALLS (option=stop-reading). On connection 1 the peer shrinks
# its receive buffer, reads the daemon's first UPDATE, then reads nothing more.
# It writes a KEEPALIVE every second and never closes the connection itself: it
# lets go only when its own KEEPALIVE write fails, which happens once the
# DAEMON has closed the socket. Connection 2 therefore exists only because the
# daemon dropped the session. A daemon that keeps the session leaves the peer
# holding connection 1 until the test times out, and the peer fails with "the
# daemon never closed the connection the peer stopped reading".
#
# HOW THE SOCKET FILLS. 10.0.0.0/8 split into /24 is 65536 routes.
# group-updates is off, so each route is its own UPDATE, and the 50
# communities make each UPDATE about 250 octets: about 16 MiB in all. A blocked
# write needs the daemon's send buffer and the peer's receive buffer both full.
# The peer's is a few kilobytes by the option. The daemon's is autotuned by the
# kernel, to 4 MiB at most with Linux and macOS defaults, so 16 MiB cannot fit.
# A host whose send buffer ceiling is raised above that volume never blocks the
# write: the daemon then keeps the session, connection 2 never comes, and the
# test FAILS on its timeout. It cannot pass there for the wrong reason.
#
# STATE, NOT TIME. Nothing here waits a duration. The peer's script is the
# barrier: connection 2 is accepted only after the daemon closed connection 1,
# and the test ends when connection 2 has delivered the three routes below.
# The 3 s budget and the 5 s connect-retry only set how long that takes.
#
# What connection 2 proves, and what it does not: the first three routes of the
# restarted initial sync arrive, in order, on the new connection. The peer
# stops there, so the other 65533 are not checked one by one.

# The write budget, short so the run is. Must live OUTSIDE the stdin=peer
# block: option=env is consumed by the test runner, not ze-peer.
option=env:var=ze.fwd.write.deadline:value=3s

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=2
option=stop-reading:value=true:conn=1
option=timeout:value=60s

# Connection 1: the first route, then the peer reads nothing more.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:announced=180A0000

# Connection 2, read normally: the initial sync starts again from the first
# route.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=1:announced=180A0000
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=2:announced=180A0001
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=3:announced=180A0002
EOF_PEER

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	filter {
		import {
			final accept
		}
		export {
			final accept
		}
	}
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		timer { connect-retry 5; }

		update {
			attribute {
				origin igp
				local-preference 100
				next-hop 10.0.0.1
				community [ 65000:1 65000:2 65000:3 65000:4 65000:5 65000:6 65000:7 65000:8 65000:9 65000:10 65000:11 65000:12 65000:13 65000:14 65000:15 65000:16 65000:17 65000:18 65000:19 65000:20 65000:21 65000:22 65000:23 65000:24 65000:25 65000:26 65000:27 65000:28 65000:29 65000:30 65000:31 65000:32 65000:33 65000:34 65000:35 65000:36 65000:37 65000:38 65000:39 65000:40 65000:41 65000:42 65000:43 65000:44 65000:45 65000:46 65000:47 65000:48 65000:49 65000:50 ]
				split /24
			}
			nlri {
				ipv4/unicast add 10.0.0.0/8
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer

cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=60s
# The witness that the stop-reading option was in force and that the DAEMON
# ended connection 1 (AC-10). ze logs this WARN line only when a write to the
# peer waited out the whole write budget, which happens only while the peer
# reads nothing with its connection open. A peer on which the option is not in
# force reads everything and closes connection 1 itself: ze dials again on its
# retry timer, connection 2 passes, and ze never logs the line. This draft
# passed that way on 2026-11-14, when the option was parsed and then dropped
# before it reached the peer.
#
# budget=3s shows the budget set through the environment above is the one the
# blocked write carried: the default is 30s.
#
# The witness is ze's log and not the peer's own lines ("reading nothing more
# on this connection"), because the runner keeps a test peer's output apart
# from the command streams: it is in no command's stdout or stderr span, so no
# expect= line can read it.
expect=stderr:contains=the peer accepted no data for the write budget
expect=stderr:contains=code=doctor-bgp-peer-not-reading
expect=stderr:contains=budget=3s

reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
